\title{PsyML: Machine Learning Workflows for Supervised, Unsupervised, and Causal
Analysis}
\description{
PsyML is organized around three approaches to machine learning with
tabular data, each run by a single function that returns an object with
\code{print()}, \code{summary()}, \code{plot()} and \code{predict()} methods.
}
\section{Supervised learning (predicting a known outcome)}{

\code{\link[=supervised_ml]{supervised_ml()}} fits and compares classification or regression models
for an outcome that is already observed. It covers exploratory checks,
preprocessing, cross-validated training of several models, hyperparameter
tuning, test-set evaluation, threshold optimization, calibration, fairness
and decision curve analysis, and interpretation with variable importance
and Shapley values. Each step is also available as a separate function:
\code{\link[=preprocess_data]{preprocess_data()}}, \code{\link[=train_models]{train_models()}}, \code{\link[=compare_models]{compare_models()}},
\code{\link[=tune_best_model]{tune_best_model()}}, \code{\link[=evaluate_model]{evaluate_model()}}, \code{\link[=optimize_threshold]{optimize_threshold()}},
\code{\link[=analyze_fairness]{analyze_fairness()}}, \code{\link[=analyze_dca]{analyze_dca()}} and \code{\link[=interpret_model]{interpret_model()}}. See the
"Other supervised learning" links in each help page.
}

\section{Unsupervised learning (finding structure without an outcome)}{

\code{\link[=unsupervised_ml]{unsupervised_ml()}} looks for groups and unusual cases when there is no
outcome variable. It compares k-means, hierarchical, DBSCAN, Gaussian
mixture and latent class clustering, chooses the number of clusters,
reduces dimensionality (PCA, t-SNE, UMAP) and detects anomalies (isolation
forest, local outlier factor, Mahalanobis distance). \code{\link[=cluster_profile]{cluster_profile()}}
describes the clusters found.
}

\section{Causal inference (estimating the effect of a treatment)}{

\code{\link[=causal_ml]{causal_ml()}} estimates the effect of a treatment or exposure on an
outcome from observational data. It checks overlap and balance, and
estimates average and heterogeneous effects with propensity scores
(matching, inverse probability weighting, doubly robust), meta-learners
(S, T and X), double machine learning, causal forests and targeted maximum
likelihood estimation.
}

\section{Tools shared by the three approaches}{

\itemize{
\item Study design: \code{\link[=ml_sample_size]{ml_sample_size()}} estimates the sample size needed for a
target performance from learning curves; \code{\link[=block_split]{block_split()}} and
\code{\link[=diversity_split]{diversity_split()}} build train and test sets.
\item Exploratory data analysis: \code{\link[=eda_summary]{eda_summary()}} and the other \verb{eda_*()}
functions.
\item Reporting: \code{\link[=export_verbose_txt]{export_verbose_txt()}}, \code{\link[=export_verbose_json]{export_verbose_json()}} and
\code{\link[=generate_report_from_json]{generate_report_from_json()}}.
}

The vignette \code{vignette("three-approaches", package = "PsyML")} runs a short
example of each approach.
